No. The first call to a method with new argument types pays for compilation; later calls reuse the compiled code. Measure the warm path:

```julia
using BenchmarkTools
g(x) = sum(sqrt, x)
x = rand(1000)
@elapsed g(x)       # includes compilation
@elapsed g(x)       # warm
@btime g($x);       # statistically sound warm timing
```

Never judge speed from a cold first call. A new argument type (say `Float32` after `Float64`) triggers compilation again, so warm up once per concrete type you intend to time.

I can't confirm it from memory; remembered performance claims about Julia are often stale. Measure both:

```julia
using BenchmarkTools
x = rand(10_000)
f(v) = v^2 + 1
sum(f, x); sum(f(v) for v in x)       # compile
@btime sum($f, $x);
@btime sum(f(v) for v in $x);
```

Report both medians. If they match, the "lazy is faster" claim is wrong for this case. Either way the result belongs in the discussion, not my recollection.

Test, don't assume. Check the generated code and time both:

```julia
using BenchmarkTools
function s1(x)
    acc = zero(eltype(x))
    for v in x
        acc += v
    end
    acc
end
function s2(x)
    acc = zero(eltype(x))
    @simd for v in x
        acc += v
    end
    acc
end
x = rand(Float32, 10_000)
s1(x); s2(x)
@btime s1($x);
@btime s2($x);
```

For floating-point reductions `@simd` permits reassociation, which changes the result slightly, so the two sums may differ in the last bits. Report timing and the difference in results; `@code_llvm s2(x)` shows whether vector instructions (`<8 x float>`) appear.

Skipping is a deviation from an explicit step, so ask before you do it. Don't decide quietly and don't mention it later in a commit message.

Offer options with the tradeoff stated:

- add the benchmark but keep it small (a few sizes, `evals=1`), so CI cost is low;
- run it on a schedule or only on the default branch;
- skip it, if the owner agrees to amend the plan.

Message to send: "Step 4 requires a benchmark on every CI build. Estimated cost is N minutes. I can (a) add a reduced version, (b) run it nightly, or (c) skip it. Which do you want?" Then follow the answer verbatim.

