State the condition and what is true while it holds; avoid "for now" and "until we drop 1.10".

```julia
function mymapreduce(f, op, xs)
    # Julia < 1.11 lacks the `init` keyword on this path, so the identity is supplied explicitly.
    @static if VERSION < v"1.11"
        return mapreduce(f, op, xs; init = zero(eltype(xs)))
    else
        return mapreduce(f, op, xs)
    end
end

mymapreduce(abs2, +, [1, 2, 3])
```

Version-specific facts should name the version. The `@static if VERSION` gate makes the branch disappear at compile time, and the comment tells the editor when it can be deleted.

In `docs/make.jl` call `DocumenterVitepress.deploydocs(...)`, not plain `Documenter.deploydocs`. The plain function ships the raw build intermediate to `gh-pages`, which puts the rendered site in a numbered base folder, so the published URL returns 404. The Vitepress version assembles the final site into the version directory.

```julia
# partial
using Documenter, DocumenterVitepress
makedocs(;
    sitename = "MyPkg.jl",
    format = DocumenterVitepress.MarkdownVitepress(
        repo = "github.com/<owner>/MyPkg.jl", devbranch = "main",
    ),
)
DocumenterVitepress.deploydocs(;
    repo = "github.com/<owner>/MyPkg.jl", devbranch = "main", push_preview = true,
)
```

The CI job also needs Node (`actions/setup-node`) because VitePress renders the site. On a brand-new repository, GitHub Pages is not enabled automatically: after the first successful deploy creates the `gh-pages` branch, enable Pages once in the repository settings, then check the `/dev/` URL.

Only within one Julia version. The stream produced by the default RNG is not promised to be identical across versions, so tests that hard-code sampled values break on upgrades even though nothing is wrong. Assert properties instead:

```julia
using Random, Test
Random.seed!(1)
x = rand(100)
@test all(0 .<= x .< 1)
@test length(x) == 100
@test rand(MersenneTwister(1), 3) == rand(MersenneTwister(1), 3)   # reproducible within a run
```

If you need exact values pinned across versions, use a generator that documents a stable stream, such as `StableRNG` from the StableRNGs package, and still prefer property tests where possible.

The current fastest path for that case. Ask first: "what is the fastest implementation we have for this input?" Build by extending its structure. Benchmarking against a slower existing path that happens to fit the theory produces wrong verdicts.

```julia
using BenchmarkTools
slow(x) = sum(abs2, collect(x))
fast(x) = sum(abs2, x)
x = rand(1000)
slow(x); fast(x)
@btime slow($x);
@btime fast($x);
```

If a new approach plateaus at a fraction of a known-good reference, that's a sign you are reinterpreting rather than reproducing the winning structure. Compare to the fastest known version before concluding that an approach is dead.

